From 6916618843c66e27add224a03eb12b46421f3d1e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 15:59:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] Filter trees, a brute-force oracle and an indexed engine
 for streamlines

Step C0 of the viewer platform's filter plan: ground truth for TrackVis-style
filtering, before any browser engine exists to be tested against it.

zarr_vectors_tools.filtering (numpy only, so it also runs under Pyodide):
- predicates: regions (sphere, box, slab, disk with an angle window, voxel
  mask), leaves (passes / column / group) and and/or/not, with a versioned
  JSON form ("zvf-predicate" v1) that other engines will read.  The module
  docstring pins down each region's exact meaning.
- oracle: tests every segment; defines the answer.  Plus summaries and
  histograms over accepted streamlines.
- IndexedEngine: segments filed under a cell grid, leaves cached by content,
  so an operator flip is only boolean algebra.  Held to the oracle exactly
  by property tests over random regions and trees, at cell sizes that make
  segments span and skip cells.
- Tractogram / load_tractogram (level 0 in the RAS mm select_streamlines
  uses) and whole_brain_like synthetic sets.

select_streamlines missed a streamline whose segment crosses the region
with no vertex inside it and one end in a chunk the region does not touch:
the read was cropped to the touched chunks, which drops that segment.  The
oracle found it on the first store test.  It now also reads the chunks
within `reach` of the region (default: one chunk on every side); a store's
step length as `reach` gives the same answer from fewer chunks.

benchmarks/filtering: gate G1 of the platform build guide, the same
measurement under CPython and under Pyodide in Node.

